1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,105
Good evening from the OROS closing desk.

2
00:00:03,105 --> 00:00:09,005
This morning the system named no pick, so tonight there is nothing to grade.

3
00:00:09,005 --> 00:00:13,740
No setup, no trade, no loss — that is the discipline working.

4
00:00:13,740 --> 00:00:18,676
Why we grade in public: a forecast that is never checked is just content.

5
00:00:18,676 --> 00:00:24,085
Every morning call gets an evening verdict, and both go in the permanent record.

6
00:00:24,050 --> 00:00:27,170
On the day, the book is up 352 dollars.

7
00:00:27,170 --> 00:00:29,890
NVIDIA (NVDA) leads, +3.0 percent.

8
00:00:29,890 --> 00:00:32,530
General Motors (GM) lags at -2.9.

9
00:00:32,530 --> 00:00:36,850
The checklist is clear: nothing requires action today.

10
00:00:36,850 --> 00:00:40,377
On screen, the day's gainers and laggards inside the paper book.

11
00:00:40,333 --> 00:00:47,900
And here is the book itself — the actual holdings, identical to this morning's emailed report.

12
00:00:47,900 --> 00:00:58,042
12 positions, led by NVIDIA, ticker NVDA, State Street Technology Select Sector SPDR ETF, ticker XLK, GE Aerospace, ticker GE.

13
00:00:58,042 --> 00:01:08,989
Every line carries its share count, its entry, and its exit — because a position without a defined exit is not a position, it is a hope.

14
00:01:08,940 --> 00:01:13,611
Now the part that makes this system different: the loop.

15
00:01:13,611 --> 00:01:15,946
Every morning OROS proposes.

16
00:01:15,946 --> 00:01:26,205
Every evening it grades its own call, journals the verdict, and adjusts its own risk before it is allowed to propose again.

17
00:01:26,205 --> 00:01:30,042
Inside the book today: 9 positions up, 3 down.

18
00:01:30,042 --> 00:01:32,378
Best, NVDA at +3.03 percent.

19
00:01:32,378 --> 00:01:33,962
Worst, GM at -2.90.

20
00:01:33,962 --> 00:01:38,800
Both go in the journal — wins and losses get the same ink.

21
00:01:38,800 --> 00:01:45,723
The honest scoreboard reads: expectancy -8.37% per trade over 176 graded positions.

22
00:01:45,723 --> 00:01:52,562
Because that record is soft, the system has throttled itself to 25% position size.

23
00:01:52,562 --> 00:01:57,567
Nobody told it to — full size must be earned back with wins.

24
00:01:57,567 --> 00:02:12,914
What tonight's read hands to tomorrow's open: 251 names close the day in Stage 2; 2 new invalidations queue exit reviews; position size stays cut to 25% until the record earns it back.

25
00:02:12,887 --> 00:02:15,104
Over to the sports desk.

26
00:02:15,104 --> 00:02:24,252
Day 28 of the race: three books, the same one hundred thousand simulated dollars, since July sixth.

27
00:02:24,252 --> 00:02:30,442
In first place, the live self-rebuilding book, in blue, at 104,153.

28
00:02:30,442 --> 00:02:35,893
Then the index fund, in gray, 1.3 thousand back at 102,823.

29
00:02:35,893 --> 00:02:43,100
Then the frozen first portfolio, the green line, 3.4 thousand back at 100,740.

30
00:02:43,100 --> 00:02:45,687
The race has no finish line.

31
00:02:45,687 --> 00:02:49,937
Same books, same dollars, back at it tomorrow.

32
00:02:49,937 --> 00:02:54,742
Every line starts the same day with the same dollar.

33
00:02:54,742 --> 00:02:56,590
Nothing is restated.

34
00:02:56,590 --> 00:03:05,498
On screen, three equity lines grown from the same starting dollar: the frozen first book, the live book, and the index fund.

35
00:03:05,460 --> 00:03:12,795
All figures are simulated paper trading, graded and journaled by the OROS agents with no human intervention.

36
00:03:12,795 --> 00:03:15,036
Nothing here is financial advice.

37
00:03:15,036 --> 00:03:18,025
From the closing desk — see you at the open.
